Subject: Keep the cv-qualifiers an elaborated type carries

A cv-qualified referent written with a qualified name loses the qualifier.
`void f(const std::int32_t&)` comes out `*mut c_int`, a mutable pointer to
something C++ will not let anyone write to, while the `const int&` beside it
comes out `*const c_int` as it should. `void f(volatile std::uint32_t&)` loses
more: the qualifier has no Rust spelling at any depth, so the marker newtype is
all that carries it, and without one nothing downstream can tell the parameter
from a reference to ordinary storage. Anything which takes the address of such
a function - a member pointer, a function pointer - then names a signature the
function does not have, so the loss is not cosmetic.

clang wraps a qualified name in `CXType_Elaborated`, and `Type::from_clang_ty`
deals with one by recursing on `clang_Type_getNamedType`. That call answers
with the type the name denotes and none of the cv-qualifiers the elaborated
node carries, so the recursion sees a bare `int32_t`. Every reader of the
qualifiers sits downstream of that point - the tail of `from_clang_ty` takes
both flags from whichever type the recursion ended on, `TryToRustTy` renders a
pointer or a reference `*const` or `*mut` from the pointee item's `is_const`
and reads `is_volatile` off the same item to place the marker - so both
qualifiers are gone for good. The same alias named without its namespace is not
elaborated at all, which is why only one of the two spellings was wrong.

Put whichever qualifier the recursion dropped back on what it produced. A newly
parsed type is this use's own node and can carry them directly. A type which
resolved to an existing item cannot: that item is shared with every other use
of the name, most of them qualified by neither, so the qualifiers go on a
`ResolvedTypeRef` wrapper instead - which is what wrapping is for here, and
what clang's own qualified nodes look like. `build_ty_wrapper` is the
constructor which takes both flags off the clang type it is handed, so it
reports the elaborated node as it stands; `build_const_wrapper` beside it
forces `const` on, which would misdescribe a node qualified only `volatile`.

--- a/ir/ty.rs
+++ b/ir/ty.rs
@@ -1451,13 +1451,57 @@
                     );
                 }
                 CXType_Elaborated => {
-                    return Self::from_clang_ty(
+                    // `clang_Type_getNamedType` answers with the type the name
+                    // denotes and none of the cv-qualifiers the elaborated node
+                    // carries: `const std::int32_t` names a plain `int32_t`.
+                    // The tail of this function reads both flags off whichever
+                    // type the recursion ended on, so recursing alone drops
+                    // them - a `const` pointee reaches codegen as `*mut T`, a
+                    // `volatile` one with no marker to say so.
+                    let named = ty.named();
+                    let result = Self::from_clang_ty(
                         potential_id,
-                        &ty.named(),
+                        &named,
                         location,
                         parent_id,
                         ctx,
-                    );
+                    )?;
+                    let lost_const = ty.is_const() && !named.is_const();
+                    let lost_volatile =
+                        ty.is_volatile() && !named.is_volatile();
+                    if !lost_const && !lost_volatile {
+                        return Ok(result);
+                    }
+                    return Ok(match result {
+                        ParseResult::New(mut inner, decl) => {
+                            inner.is_const |= lost_const;
+                            let is_volatile =
+                                inner.is_volatile() || lost_volatile;
+                            ParseResult::New(
+                                inner.with_volatile(is_volatile),
+                                decl,
+                            )
+                        }
+                        // The name resolved to an item every other use of it
+                        // shares, so the qualifiers cannot be written onto that
+                        // item; they go on a wrapper, which is what wrapping
+                        // exists for here. `build_ty_wrapper` reports the
+                        // elaborated node's own qualifiers, where
+                        // `build_const_wrapper` would force `const` onto a node
+                        // qualified only `volatile`.
+                        ParseResult::AlreadyResolved(id) => {
+                            let with_id = ctx.next_item_id();
+                            ParseResult::AlreadyResolved(
+                                ctx.build_ty_wrapper(
+                                    with_id,
+                                    id.as_type_id_unchecked(),
+                                    parent_id,
+                                    ty,
+                                )
+                                .into(),
+                            )
+                        }
+                    });
                 }
                 CXType_ObjCId => TypeKind::ObjCId,
                 CXType_ObjCSel => TypeKind::ObjCSel,
